WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
It's time for a new puzzle of the month.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.649
Suppose you have two spheres, each of radius 1,

00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:06.550
positioned randomly in space,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.100
but you know that they intersect.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.300
What is the expected volume of that intersection?

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.050
Or phrased another way,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.448
you could say one sphere is

00:00:14.448 --> 00:00:15.350
centered at the origin,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.632
and you choose a random point within

00:00:17.632 --> 00:00:19.266
a distance 2 of the origin to be

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:20.900
the center of that second sphere.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.500
This guarantees that the two spheres intersect,

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.193
although where they intersect is

00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:26.550
some hard to think about shape.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.842
Depending on where that second sphere ended up.

00:00:28.842 --> 00:00:30.234
And again, the question is,

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:33.084
what is the expected volume of that shape?

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:35.659
By the way, I've been saving up solutions to

00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:37.639
several of these monthly puzzles to package

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:39.709
together into fuller videos that I'll publish

00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:41.734
near the end of the year during the holidays.

00:00:41.884 --> 00:00:44.013
In the meantime, a few previews are available

00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:44.984
to Patreon supporters.

